# Reminder-only beat for demo step 1b: protolens on a clean capture.
#
# The view stays rooted (`node: /`) throughout, so it never scrolls out
# from under the reader. The message opens fully unfolded; two steps then
# fold the big `grid` field down (level 1, then level 0) until the three
# top-level fields — `grid`, `rules`, `generation` — fit on screen
# together, and the last steps point at each with `search`. The presenter
# still runs the interactive gestures (`w`, `v`, `z`, `a`) live. Advance
# with space or Enter.
#
# Step text is written for a 120-column line (spec 0400).
#
# Invocation (from grehack2026.sh):
#   protolens --descriptor-set life.desc capture/000001-request.pb \
#       --script beats/capture

title: Reading one message with protolens

steps:
  - text: |
      The capture, opened against life.desc.

      protolens renders it as textproto, enriched with annotations and it inferred the type: StepRequest.

      - arrow keys / mouse to move
      - `z` or the triangles to fold / unfold a message
      - hover over the root type for its full name and score
      👋 Press space.
    node: /

  - text: |
      Fully expanded. A StepRequest has three top-level fields — the first, highlighted,
      is `grid`: a Grid message, rows of cells, and most of what is on screen.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /
    search: /1

  - text: |
      `grid` folds down one level — its rows collapse to a single line each, the whole grid now a short block.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /
    fold: ["/1 1"]
    search: /1

  - text: |
      And fully: `grid` is now one line. The three top-level fields fit on screen together.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /
    fold: ["/1 0"]
    search: /1

  - text: |
      The second field is `rules` — another nested message, with the birth and survival ranges.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /
    fold: ["/1 0"]
    search: /2

  - text: |
      The third is `generation` — a scalar `uint64`, sitting just below `rules`. Drop
      to wire level with `w`: the raw bytes, the tag, the wire type, the varint groups.

      👋 Press `w` on `generation`, then `w` again to close it.
    node: /
    fold: ["/1 0"]
    search: /3

  - text: |
      On a field whose type is a named message or enum (not a bare scalar), press `v`.

      protolens hands off to Neovim, opened at that type's declaration in the reconstructed .proto source —
      the SourceCodeInfo reproto synthesized, resolved against the proto/ stub beside life.desc.

      👋 Press `v` on `grid` or `rules`, look at the declaration, come back.
    node: /
    fold: ["/1 0"]
    search: /1

  - text: |
      That is the whole loop: a type recovered from the binary, a message read in the clear, the wire underneath one
      keystroke away — and from a byte on the wire to the line of reconstructed source that defines it, and back.

      👋 Type `:q` to quit protolens when you are done.
    node: /
